From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AltarsCN <337293323+Altars3668@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 21 Sep 2026 07:09:37 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] win32u: a stored display config that names a monitor we did
 not find is not readable.

update_display_cache_from_registry linked each source to its monitors by path
and quietly skipped the ones it could not find, then reported success as long
as neither list had come out empty.  What that leaves behind is a source with
no monitor and a monitor with no source -- and a monitor with no source answers
every question the way the service window station's stand-in does: a 1024x768
primary called WinDisc.  Since the read had "succeeded", nothing ever went back
to the driver to correct it.

From the desktop that looks like the display shrinking for no reason.  Swap the
screen under a running session -- here a remote-desktop virtual output replaced
by the real 3840x2160 panel -- and the config left behind still names the old
monitor's device path.  Every process started afterwards is told the screen is
1024x768, and windows size themselves to match: Word came up 1036 wide, with
SM_CXMAXTRACK at 1036x780, and could not be resized or maximised past it.

Fail the read instead, so the caller re-derives the devices from the driver and
writes the registry back.  After that the same probe reports 3840x2160 with
SM_CXMAXTRACK 3852x2172 and the monitor named \\.\DISPLAY1.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
(cherry picked from commit 0b9ccfd3a566f05ed68a4fd9b3b8bd937d55f544)

diff --git a/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c b/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
index c85eea84e66..3d4eaffc05d 100644
--- a/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/sysparams.c
@@ -2585,7 +2585,7 @@ static BOOL update_display_cache_from_registry( UINT64 serial )
     NTSTATUS status;
     struct gpu *gpu;
     HKEY hkey;
-    BOOL ret;
+    BOOL ret, stale = FALSE;
 
     /* If user driver didn't initialize the registry, then exit */
     if (!enum_key && !(enum_key = reg_open_ascii_key( NULL, enum_keyA )))
@@ -2645,7 +2645,22 @@ static BOOL update_display_cache_from_registry( UINT64 serial )
             struct monitor *monitor;
 
             if (!read_source_monitor_path( hkey, monitor_id, path )) break;
-            if (!(monitor = find_monitor_from_path( path ))) continue;
+            if (!(monitor = find_monitor_from_path( path )))
+            {
+                /* The stored config names a monitor this enumeration did not
+                 * produce, so what is in the registry describes a screen that
+                 * is no longer attached.  Carrying on leaves the source with no
+                 * monitor and some monitor with no source -- and a monitor with
+                 * no source answers as a 1024x768 primary called WinDisc,
+                 * because that is the shape the service window station's stand-in
+                 * has.  Every window in the session is then told the screen is
+                 * 1024x768 and sized to match, which is what a display swapped
+                 * underneath a running session looks like from the desktop.
+                 * Treat it as a failed read so the caller re-derives the config
+                 * from the driver, which then rewrites the registry. */
+                stale = TRUE;
+                continue;
+            }
 
             monitor->id = monitor_id;
             monitor->source = source_acquire( source );
@@ -2656,7 +2671,7 @@ static BOOL update_display_cache_from_registry( UINT64 serial )
         NtClose( hkey );
     }
 
-    if ((ret = !list_empty( &sources ) && !list_empty( &monitors )))
+    if ((ret = !stale && !list_empty( &sources ) && !list_empty( &monitors )))
         last_query_display_time = key.LastWriteTime.QuadPart;
 
     set_winstation_monitors( FALSE );
